Subject: [PATCH] kms: finish the teardown when reopen_device fails

`reopen_device` tears the old device down before it constructs the replacement:
it `shift_remove`s the device from `backend.drm_devices`, drops its surfaces and
calls `syncobj_state.close_device()`. `Device::new` is then called with `?`, so
if it fails the function returns with all of that already done and none of the
end-of-function cleanup performed.

The caller cannot repair it. `resume_session` responds to a failed re-open by
calling `device_removed`, but that resolves its `DrmNode` by iterating
`backend.drm_devices` -- from which the device has just been removed -- so it
fails with "Couldn't find drm node for {dev}" before removing anything:

    Failed to update drm device /dev/dri/card0. Re-opening
    Failed to re-open drm device /dev/dri/card0. Device lost
    Failed to close drm device /dev/dri/card0.

Two pieces of state are left dangling, and both outlive the failure for the rest
of the session:

  * The connectors in `old_state.outputs` are never withdrawn, so they stay
    advertised as `wl_output` globals with no device behind them. A later
    `device_added` for the same card adds a second global per connector. On a
    hybrid Intel+NVIDIA laptop that resumed without its iGPU this showed up as
    two `eDP-1` globals at conflicting positions (0,927 and 7040,0), the internal
    panel being unreachable, and no way to address either one by name.

  * The syncobj manager keeps its global while its device is closed, so every
    client that imports a timeline gets
    "wp_linux_drm_syncobj_manager_v1: error 1: failed to import syncobj timeline:
    No device". Chromium treats a Wayland protocol error as fatal and calls
    `__builtin_trap()`, so all Electron apps died at launch (SIGTRAP) until the
    session was restarted.

Finish the teardown on the failure path: withdraw the orphaned outputs the same
way the success path and `device_removed` do, and withdraw the syncobj global
rather than leaving it bound to nothing -- the same fallback already used below
when the replacement device turns out not to support syncobj eventfds, after
which clients fall back to implicit sync.

This does not try to make the re-open succeed; the device is genuinely gone at
that point. It confines the damage to the device that was lost, so the rest of
the session stays usable and a later hotplug or VT switch can add the card back
cleanly.

Note the `?` on `new_device.enumerate_surfaces()` and on `populate_modes` further
down have the same shape, but leak less: by then `new_device` exists and the
syncobj device has been handed over. They are left alone here to keep the change
reviewable.

diff --git a/src/backend/kms/device.rs b/src/backend/kms/device.rs
index 418a16f3..2109e806 100644
--- a/src/backend/kms/device.rs
+++ b/src/backend/kms/device.rs
@@ -447,14 +447,69 @@ impl State {
                 }
             };
 
-            let mut new_device = Device::new(
+            let mut new_device = match Device::new(
                 dev,
                 path,
                 &mut backend.session,
                 &mut self.common,
                 dh,
                 Some(reusable),
-            )?;
+            ) {
+                Ok(new_device) => new_device,
+                Err(err) => {
+                    // Everything above already tore the old device down: it was
+                    // `shift_remove`d from `backend.drm_devices`, its surfaces were
+                    // dropped and the syncobj manager's device was closed. Returning
+                    // here without undoing that leaves the compositor in a state that
+                    // poisons the whole session, and the caller cannot clean up for us:
+                    // `device_removed` finds its `DrmNode` by iterating
+                    // `backend.drm_devices`, so it fails with "Couldn't find drm node"
+                    // before it removes anything.
+                    //
+                    // Two things must be finished by hand.
+                    //
+                    // First the outputs. On the success path the connectors left in
+                    // `old_state.outputs` are withdrawn at the end of this function. Skip
+                    // that and they stay advertised as `wl_output` globals with no device
+                    // behind them; a later `device_added` for the same card then adds a
+                    // *second* global for each connector. Observed as two `eDP-1` outputs
+                    // at conflicting positions, one of them unreachable.
+                    let outputs_removed =
+                        old_state.outputs.values().cloned().collect::<Vec<_>>();
+                    self.common
+                        .output_configuration_state
+                        .remove_heads(outputs_removed.iter());
+                    if backend.session.is_active() {
+                        for output in outputs_removed {
+                            self.common.remove_output(&output);
+                        }
+                    } else {
+                        self.common.output_configuration_state.update();
+                    }
+
+                    // Then the syncobj manager. Its device was closed above and there is
+                    // no new device to hand it, so leaving the global up makes every
+                    // client that imports a timeline fail with
+                    // "failed to import syncobj timeline: No device". Chromium treats that
+                    // protocol error as fatal and calls `__builtin_trap()`, so Electron
+                    // apps die on launch for the rest of the session. Withdraw the global
+                    // instead, exactly as the branch below does when the replacement
+                    // device turns out not to support syncobj eventfds, and clients fall
+                    // back to implicit sync.
+                    if let Some(guard) = syncobj_guard {
+                        drop(guard);
+                        if let Some(syncobj_state) = backend.syncobj_state.take() {
+                            dh.remove_global::<State>(syncobj_state.into_global());
+                        }
+                    }
+
+                    if let Err(err) = backend.refresh_used_devices() {
+                        warn!(?err, "Failed to refresh used devices after a failed re-open");
+                    }
+
+                    return Err(err);
+                }
+            };
 
             if let Some(guard) = syncobj_guard {
                 let import_device = new_device.drm.device().device_fd().clone();
